      Mining Screen Mesh: How to Choose Opening, Wire, and Material
    </h1>

    <p class="text-theme-secondary text-lg mb-8">
      Last updated: September 2026 | 12 min read
    </p>

    <!-- Answer First -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
      <p class="text-theme-primary text-lg leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Quick answer:</strong> Choose mining screen mesh by working backward from your feed: pick the aperture 1.2–1.5× the size of your d80 particle (the opening where 80% of the feed passes), then select wire material for abrasion—65Mn (16Mn) spring steel for general ore, 72A high-carbon wire for highly abrasive feeds, stainless only for corrosive or food-grade streams. Match the weave (square for grading, harp/slotted for sticky feeds), order hook edges to fit your deck's clamping system, and tension to the manufacturer's spec—typically 25–35 N/mm of screen width. Under correct tension, quality 65Mn mesh on a primary deck lasts 400–1,200 hours; running loose or under-spec wire cuts that to a quarter.
      </p>
    </div>

    <!-- TOC -->
    <div class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg p-6 mb-8">
