    <!-- Intro -->
    <section class="mb-12">
      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Screening is usually the second-largest operating cost in a crushing circuit after power. The mesh itself is a consumable, but the wrong mesh costs far more than its price: undersize contamination downgrades product, blinding stops production, and premature wire failure sends maintenance crews into the screen house weekly. Most mines can cut screening cost per ton by 20–40% simply by matching aperture, wire, and tension to the actual feed—without buying a new screen. Our <a href="/applications/mining/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">mining applications page</a> covers the full range of screening and grading products.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Decks -->
    <section id="decks" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">How Vibrating Screen Decks Use Mesh</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Inclined vibrating screens (circular or linear throw) carry the feed in a stratified bed across one or more mesh decks. Each deck has a job: the top deck scalps off oversize (grizzly bars or heavy punched plate often do this at 50–150 mm), intermediate decks split the product into size fractions, and the bottom deck makes the final cut. Mesh types change by deck duty:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Scalping deck (top):</strong> sees the largest impact and the most abrasion. Punched plate, heavy crimped mesh in 8–12 mm wire, or self-cleaning mesh is standard here. <a href="/products/perforated-metal/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">Perforated metal plates</a> handle the impact loads that woven wire cannot.</li>
        <li><strong>Grading decks (middle):</strong> woven wire mesh in 2–8 mm wire with square or slotted apertures from 5–50 mm. This is where 65Mn and 72A wire mesh does most of its work.</li>
        <li><strong>Sizing/fines deck (bottom):</strong> finer apertures (1–10 mm) with thinner wire, 1.5–3 mm, where blinding becomes the dominant problem and self-cleaning (harp/anti-blinding) mesh earns its premium.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        The critical machine parameter for mesh life is the screen's <strong>acceleration (g-force)</strong>. Higher throw throws material harder against the wire. If your screen runs above 4.5 g, standard wire fails early—spec a harder wire grade or heavier wire diameter for the same aperture.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Opening -->
    <section id="opening" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Choosing the Aperture: The 1.2–1.5× d80 Rule</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Aperture selection starts from the particle-size distribution of the feed, not the product name. The standard heuristic: the mesh aperture should be <strong>1.2–1.5 times the d80 of the desired cut</strong>, where d80 is the particle size at which 80% of the material passes. Go below 1.2× and capacity collapses and blinding starts; go above 1.5× and too much near-size material contaminates the undersize product.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Target Product Cut</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical d80 of Feed</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Recommended Aperture</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Wire Diameter</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Rail ballast / concrete aggregate 40–60 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">25–35 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">40–50 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">6.0–8.0 mm</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Road base 20–30 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">14–20 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">25–32 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">4.0–6.0 mm</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Chip 10–14 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">7–10 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">12–16 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">3.0–4.5 mm</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Manufactured sand 0–5 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">3–4 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">4–6 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.5–2.5 mm</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Dewatering / fines removal under 1 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.4–0.6 mm</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.8–1.2 mm slotted</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.8–1.2 mm</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Two adjustments to the rule. First, <strong>near-size content</strong>: if more than 20% of the feed sits within ±25% of the aperture, open up the aperture by 10% or switch to slotted apertures, because near-size particles wedge into square apertures and blind the deck. Second, <strong>moisture</strong>: feeds above 4–5% surface moisture blind square mesh regardless of aperture; switch to harp (self-cleaning) weave or add heated decks and spray water. If you are converting between aperture in mm and US mesh count for fine decks, the <a href="/tools/mesh-count-conversion/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">mesh count conversion tool</a> handles the math.
      </p>

      <div class="bg-theme-secondary bg-opacity-10 border border-theme rounded-lg p-6">
        <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-theme-primary mb-2">Field Check</h3>
        <p class="text-theme-secondary text-sm">
          Screen the actual feed across your candidate aperture and weigh the oversize and undersize. Sims and plant audits consistently find plants running apertures 15–30% off their true cut—fixing that alone usually recovers 5–15% capacity.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Material -->
    <section id="material" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Wire Material: 65Mn, 72A, and Wear-Resistant Options</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Screen wire lives in pure abrasion: quartz-rich rock grinds steel wire at 1–4 mg per ton of feed. The material must be hard enough to resist that grinding but tough enough to flex for millions of cycles without fatigue-fracturing. Chinese mills supply three workhorse grades:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Grade</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Carbon / Mn</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Hardness (HRC after oil quench)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Best For</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Relative Cost</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">65Mn (16Mn spring steel)</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.62–0.70% C, 0.90–1.20% Mn</td>
              <td class="p-3">45–50 HRC</td>
              <td class="p-3">General ore grading, most quarries</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.0×</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">72A / 82B high-carbon wire</td>
              <td class="p-3">0.72–0.85% C</td>
              <td class="p-3">50–55 HRC</td>
              <td class="p-3">Highly abrasive feeds: granite, basalt, quartzite</td>
              <td class="p-3">1.2–1.4×</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">304 / 316 stainless</td>
              <td class="p-3">18–20% Cr, 8–12% Ni</td>
              <td class="p-3">Annealed, soft</td>
              <td class="p-3">Corrosive ores, marine sand, food/pharma fines</td>
              <td class="p-3">3–5×</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Polyurethane / rubber panels</td>
              <td class="p-3">—</td>
              <td class="p-3">—</td>
              <td class="p-3">Very sticky or very wet feeds, dewatering</td>
              <td class="p-3">2–4×</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        For 90% of hard-rock applications, 65Mn at 45–50 HRC is the value optimum. 72A earns its premium when silica content exceeds 65% or when measured wear life of 65Mn drops below 400 hours. Insist on <strong>oil-tempered (quenched and tempered) wire</strong> rather than "as-drawn" wire: the tempering relieves drawing stress so the wire flexes through millions of vibration cycles without fatigue cracking—a failure mode that looks like abrasion but is actually brittleness.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Weave -->
    <section id="weave" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Weave Types: Square, Harp, and Slotted</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Plain square weave (crimped):</strong> pre-crimped wires locked at every intersection. Accurate sizing, cheapest, good open area. Default choice for dry feeds under 5% moisture.</li>
        <li><strong>Slotted (oblong) weave:</strong> rectangular apertures, typically 1.5–3:1 length-to-width. Passes 15–30% more tonnage than square at the same cut and resists near-size blinding; slightly less precise separation.</li>
        <li><strong>Harp (self-cleaning) weave:</strong> straight longitudinal wires held by widely spaced cross wires, allowing each wire to vibrate independently like a string. Wires flex and shed wedged particles—the standard fix for sticky clay, high-moisture feeds, and blinding fines decks. Wears faster than crimped mesh but keeps the deck open.</li>
        <li><strong>Piano wire (long slot harp):</strong> very thin, high-tensile wires in long slots for sand and fine gravel. Maximum open area and throughput on dewatering decks.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Match weave to the failure you are actually seeing. Blinding (material plugging apertures) → harp or slotted. Abrasive wear (wire thinning, breakage at the crimp) → harder grade or heavier wire. Pegging (stones stuck in apertures) → larger aperture or slotted geometry. Our comparison of <a href="/blog/welded-vs-woven-wire-mesh/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">welded vs woven wire mesh</a> explains why woven—never welded—mesh belongs on vibrating decks: the welds fatigue-crack under cyclic flexing.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Hooks & tensioning -->
    <section id="hooks" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Hook Edges, Tensioning, and Deck Fit</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        A screen mesh does not work unless it bolts into the deck's tensioning system. Edge preparation matters as much as the wire:
      </p>

      <ul class="list-disc list-inside text-theme-secondary space-y-2 mb-6">
        <li><strong>Hook types:</strong> angle hooks (45° or 60°) for cam-lock tensioning rails; U-hooks for tensioning bolts; hook-and-bend for clamp bars. Measure your deck's rail profile and send a sketch or photo with the order—hook geometry mismatch is the most common reason a correct mesh "doesn't fit."</li>
        <li><strong>Edge reinforcement:</strong> a double-wire selvage or an extra border wire on all four sides prevents the edge wires—the highest-stress zone—from tearing out. Cheap mesh omits this and fails at the hook within weeks.</li>
        <li><strong>Tension:</strong> tension the mesh to the screen maker's spec, typically 25–35 N per mm of screen width for woven wire. Under-tensioned mesh flexes excessively, wears at the crimps, and allows oversized near-size particles to "sail" through; over-tensioned mesh snaps wires prematurely.</li>
        <li><strong>Install practice:</strong> center the mesh, tension in stages across all bolts (not corner-to-corner), and re-tension after the first 8–24 hours of running as the mesh beds in.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Wire gauge tolerance also belongs in your purchase order. Screen wire is sold by the kg, and shaving 0.1 mm off a 4.0 mm wire removes roughly 5% of the steel—use a micrometer at inspection, or convert gauge numbers with the <a href="/tools/wire-gauge-conversion/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">wire gauge conversion tool</a> when comparing quotes that mix metric and SWG sizes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Wear life -->
    <section id="wear-life" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-4">Wear Life and Cost per Ton Screened</h2>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4 leading-relaxed">
        Price per panel is the wrong metric; cost per thousand tons screened is the right one. Typical ranges under correct tension and matched aperture:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto mb-6">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-theme-card border-b border-theme">
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Deck / Mesh Combination</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Typical Wear Life (hours)</th>
              <th class="text-left p-3 text-theme-primary font-semibold">Indicative Price (USD/m²)</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="text-theme-secondary">
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Top scalping deck, heavy crimped 65Mn</td>
              <td class="p-3">800–2,000</td>
              <td class="p-3">18–35</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Grading deck, 65Mn square weave</td>
              <td class="p-3">400–1,200</td>
              <td class="p-3">12–25</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Grading deck, 72A high-carbon</td>
              <td class="p-3">700–1,800</td>
              <td class="p-3">16–32</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="border-b border-theme">
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Fines deck, harp weave</td>
              <td class="p-3">300–800</td>
              <td class="p-3">20–40</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="p-3 font-medium">Same mesh, run under-tensioned or loose</td>
              <td class="p-3">100–300</td>
              <td class="p-3">—</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p class="text-theme-secondary mb-4">
        Track three numbers per change-out: tons screened, hours run, and failure mode (wear, breakage, blinding, hook tear). After three change-outs the pattern tells you whether to change wire grade, aperture, tension, or all three. Most plants that log this data cut screening costs 20%+ within two quarters—usually by fixing tension, not by buying different mesh.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- FAQ -->
    <section id="faq" class="mb-12">
      <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-theme-primary mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-4">
        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How do I choose the screen mesh aperture for my feed?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Use the 1.2–1.5× d80 rule: measure or estimate the particle size where 80% of the feed passes, then set the aperture at 1.2–1.5 times that value. Adjust upward if more than 20% of the feed is near-size (within ±25% of the aperture) or moisture exceeds 5%. Verify with a field trial across the candidate aperture before committing to a full order.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What is the difference between 65Mn and 72A screen wire?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            65Mn (about 0.65% carbon spring steel) reaches 45–50 HRC after oil tempering and is the value choice for general quarry and ore feeds. 72A high-carbon wire runs 50–55 HRC and resists abrasion roughly 40–70% longer on silica-rich feeds like granite and quartzite, at a 20–40% price premium. Both must be quenched and tempered—soft "as-drawn" wire of either grade fails by fatigue cracking, not abrasion.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">Why does my screen mesh keep blinding, and what fixes it?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Blinding—particles wedging in apertures—comes from near-size content, moisture above 4–5%, or an aperture too close to the d80. Fixes in order of cost: open the aperture 10%, switch square apertures to slotted, switch to harp (self-cleaning) weave so wires flex and shed particles, add spray water, or upgrade to polyurethane panels for extreme stickiness. Harp mesh wears faster but keeps the deck open and throughput stable.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">How tight should vibrating screen mesh be tensioned?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Follow the screen manufacturer's spec, typically 25–35 N per mm of screen width for woven wire on inclined vibrating screens. Under-tension causes crimp wear and lets near-size oversize sail into the undersize product; over-tension snaps wires early. Tension in stages across all bolts and re-check after the first 8–24 hours of operation as the mesh beds in.
          </div>
        </details>

        <details class="bg-theme-card border border-theme rounded-lg">
          <summary class="p-4 font-semibold text-theme-primary cursor-pointer">What should I specify when ordering mining screen mesh from a factory?</summary>
          <div class="p-4 pt-0 text-theme-secondary">
            Specify: aperture or slot size with tolerance (±5%), wire diameter with tolerance, wire grade (65Mn or 72A, oil-tempered, state hardness range), weave type, panel dimensions, hook type on each edge (send a photo of your deck rails), edge reinforcement, and target tension. Add a micrometer check and hook-geometry check to your pre-shipment inspection. Weight matters for freight—estimate panel tonnage with the <a href="/tools/woven-wire-mesh-weight-calculator/" class="text-theme-accent hover:underline">woven mesh weight calculator</a> before requesting quotes.
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

